# The native helpers a release's Windows and Mac update bundles may carry (bin/), each one you have vouched for: its
# SHA-256, the system (win32 or darwin) and its name in the bundle, one line per helper and system:
#   <sha256>  win32 bin/flick-bt.exe
# GitHub builds them from native/ (build_win.sh: flick-bt.exe, flick-keys.exe, flick-sparse.exe; build_mac.sh:
# flick-keys) and every Windows PC and Mac that updates runs them, so signing/sign_release.js signs a release only when
# each of its helpers is listed here, as this file is in your checkout.
#
# None is listed yet: the first time (and whenever native/ or the compiler CI uses changes), sign_release.js stops,
# names each helper with its SHA-256 and gives the line to put here. Before putting it here:
#   1. check that native/ in the release's commit is what you wrote (sign_release.js says which git log to read);
#   2. best, build the helper yourself the way build_win.sh or build_mac.sh does and compare its SHA-256 (the Windows
#      ones come out the same byte for byte with the same MinGW compiler: Ubuntu 24.04's gcc-mingw-w64-x86-64);
#   3. then add the line, run sign_release.js again and commit this file. A helper that later comes out different is
#      refused until you vouch for it again.
#
# 2.1.0 (2026-11-11): native/ is the same as in 2.0.0, byte for byte. The Mac helper is the one in 2.0.0's signed bundle;
# the Windows ones differ from 2.0.0's only by having no time stamp (build_win.sh, -Wl,--no-insert-timestamp) and came
# out the same from two separate builds of this code.
0de507ce23e8be75cf9e266edb7f45b42ff47750269ae0f238d3be048846152e  win32 bin/flick-bt.exe
db54f3fc69c1e2d03256903975f5693ef9a0cdb49c3c6d9ce83a37bb1fd72632  win32 bin/flick-sparse.exe
c641b44907982dc37346b2b1f97ccff02006d9ac9e755d4a3b654f1eb3be8df7  win32 bin/flick-keys.exe
0e8f06ae15524458e4ba2ef5720296d8938f6b0a43ebf16aebc85b012d6a77ce  darwin bin/flick-keys
